---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/product.css';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="AI copilot" description="An optional desktop team of AI agents collaborating across your workspace and leaving a dimension-driven, human-editable feature history. Requires a compatible runtime and your own provider account or API key; online browser Copilot is unavailable.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Product / Collaborate</p>
      <h1>A team of agents that operates the tools, not a chat window beside them.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Describe the outcome the way you'd brief a group of engineers. The agents split the job across your workspace — sketching, features, measurement, simulation, CAM — using the same tools you use by hand, in the document you have open. What they leave behind is a human-editable feature history: fully defined sketches, named relations, and the critical dimensions you specified.</p>
      <ul class="product-hero-meta">
        <li>A team of agents working in the open document</li>
        <li>A human-editable, dimension-driven feature tree</li>
        <li>Optional desktop runtime and your own provider account or API key</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="supporting-copy">Online Copilot is not available in the browser deployment. Public desktop installers and runtime availability must not be assumed; check <a href="/download/">current setup choices</a> and your source checkout's runtime instructions.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="brief-title">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow" id="brief-title">Describe the outcome</p>
        <h2>Name the part, the critical dimensions, and what must not change.</h2>
        <p class="lede">You don't need to know tool names or write JSON. A brief that states intent and constraints gets better work out of the agents than a list of button clicks, for exactly the same reason it does with colleagues.</p>
        <p class="supporting-copy">Follow-ups work best when they ask for something concrete: measure the minimum wall thickness, show the right view, check interference with the housing. Measurements and renders reduce ambiguity &mdash; they do not certify a design.</p>
        <div class="actions"><a class="button button-secondary" href="/docs/ai-copilot/">Copilot guide</a></div>
      </header>
      <div class="product-compare">
        <div class="product-compare-good">
          <p class="eyebrow">Useful brief</p>
          <p>&ldquo;Add four M4 clearance holes on a 60 mm bolt circle, centered on the top face. Do not change the outer profile.&rdquo;</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Weak brief</p>
          <p>&ldquo;Open the sketch tool, click the circle button, then pattern it around a bit.&rdquo;</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow" id="loop-title">The loop</p>
        <h2>Intent in, inspectable geometry out.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Each agent's activity appears in the copilot feed, and their geometry lands in the same feature tree you use manually — sketches fully defined, relations named. Nothing lands in a parallel, opaque model.</p>
      </header>
      <figure class="product-diagram">
        <svg class="product-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="copilot-diagram-title">
          <title id="copilot-diagram-title">The copilot loop: a plain-language brief drives real modeling tools, the geometry lands in the open document's feature tree, and you review it before saving a revision.</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="copilot-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path class="product-svg-arrow-accent" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

          <rect class="product-svg-node-accent" x="60" y="40" width="180" height="70" rx="4" />
          <text class="product-svg-title" x="80" y="70">INTENT</text>
          <text class="product-svg-label" x="80" y="92">plain-language brief</text>

          <rect class="product-svg-node" x="400" y="40" width="180" height="70" rx="4" />
          <text class="product-svg-title" x="420" y="70">TOOLS</text>
          <text class="product-svg-label" x="420" y="92">sketches + features</text>

          <rect class="product-svg-node" x="400" y="190" width="180" height="70" rx="4" />
          <text class="product-svg-title" x="420" y="220">MODEL</text>
          <text class="product-svg-label" x="420" y="242">same feature tree</text>

          <rect class="product-svg-node" x="60" y="190" width="180" height="70" rx="4" />
          <text class="product-svg-title" x="80" y="220">REVIEW</text>
          <text class="product-svg-label" x="80" y="242">measure, then save</text>

          <line class="product-svg-flow" x1="250" y1="75" x2="392" y2="75" marker-end="url(#copilot-arrow)" />
          <line class="product-svg-flow" x1="490" y1="120" x2="490" y2="182" marker-end="url(#copilot-arrow)" />
          <line class="product-svg-flow" x1="390" y1="225" x2="250" y2="225" marker-end="url(#copilot-arrow)" />
          <line class="product-svg-flow" x1="150" y1="180" x2="150" y2="118" marker-end="url(#copilot-arrow)" />

          <rect class="product-svg-con" x="255" y="128" width="130" height="44" rx="4" />
          <text class="product-svg-label-dim" x="320" y="155" text-anchor="middle">OPEN DOCUMENT</text>

          <text class="product-svg-label-faint" x="320" y="62" text-anchor="middle">REQUEST</text>
          <text class="product-svg-label-faint" x="320" y="246" text-anchor="middle">EVIDENCE</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Every pass ends with your judgment, not with the copilot's confidence.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ul class="product-tiles product-stack">
        <li class="product-tile"><strong>Inspect geometry</strong><span>Read the open model rather than guessing at it.</span></li>
        <li class="product-tile"><strong>Create and edit features</strong><span>Ordinary, fully defined sketches and features in your feature tree.</span></li>
        <li class="product-tile"><strong>Render views</strong><span>Alternate views to show what actually changed.</span></li>
        <li class="product-tile"><strong>Measure results</strong><span>Concrete numbers you can check against intent.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="review-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow" id="review-title">After a change</p>
        <h2>Four checks before a revision is worth saving.</h2>
      </header>
      <ol class="product-steps">
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Rotate and look</h3>
            <p>Inspect the highlighted feature in the 3D view. Geometry that reads correctly from one angle can be wrong from another.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Measure the critical dimensions</h3>
            <p>Use <strong>Evaluate</strong> on the dimensions that actually matter for fit, strength, or process.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Open the edited feature</h3>
            <p>Confirm its references. A feature attached to the wrong face is a problem that surfaces two revisions later.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Save a revision when it matches your intent</h3>
            <p>Not before. If the run went the wrong direction, stop it, explain the mismatch in plain language, and point at the last good feature or revision instead of stacking corrective edits on geometry you no longer trust.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="providers-title">
    <div class="wrap product-columns">
      <div>
        <header class="section-heading product-subhead">
          <p class="eyebrow" id="providers-title">Provider connection</p>
          <h2>Your provider. Your account. Your bill.</h2>
        </header>
        <p class="product-body">In a desktop build with its compatible copilot runtime configured, open <strong>Settings → Providers</strong> and connect a supported provider account or your own API key. The prompt, project context needed for that turn, and tool results are sent to the provider you select under its privacy terms.</p>
        <p class="product-body">Your provider's plan, API prices, usage limits, and privacy terms apply. Chudware doesn't sell tokens or resell model capacity.</p>
        <p class="product-body">Treat every provider credential as a secret. Enter it only in Provider settings—never in chat, a prompt, or a project note.</p>
        <p class="product-body"><a class="text-link" href="/docs/ai-copilot/">Provider setup guide <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-spec-wrap">
        <table class="product-spec">
          <caption>Connection choices</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col"><span class="product-visually-hidden">Aspect</span></th><th scope="col">Provider account</th><th scope="col">API key</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Setup</th><td>Authorize a supported account from Provider settings</td><td>Enter a key issued by the provider in Provider settings</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Usage</th><td>Uses that account's plan and limits</td><td>Uses that provider project's API rates and limits</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Charges</th><td>Your provider charges the account owner</td><td>Your provider charges the API account owner</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Prompts</th><td>Sent only to the provider you select for the turn</td><td>Sent only to the provider you select for the turn</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="product-note product-note-warning product-stack product-span-all">
        <p class="eyebrow">Machine actions stay deliberate</p>
        <p>The copilot may prepare CAM operations and output, but anything that affects a physical machine requires explicit operator review and control. Safety, tolerance, material, and manufacturing decisions remain yours.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/product/cam/">Manufacturing hand-off <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="wrap section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next</p>
      <h2>Try it against a model you can check.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Check desktop setup and runtime prerequisites, build a first part by hand, then ask the configured copilot to revise it. Browser self-hosting does not enable online Copilot.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/download/">See setup choices</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/docs/first-part/">Build your first part</a></div>
      <ul class="product-jump">
        <li><a href="/product/">All capabilities</a></li>
        <li><a href="/product/modeling/">Modeling</a></li>
        <li><a href="/product/assemblies/">Assemblies</a></li>
        <li><a href="/product/plm/">PLM</a></li>
        <li><a href="/download/">Setup choices</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
